  <section id="istochnik">
    <h2>Where back pain comes from</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Muscles</strong> — spasm and strain, inflammation where muscles and ligaments attach. The most common cause.</li>
      <li><strong>Facet joints</strong> — small joints between the vertebrae. With arthritis, pain increases on extension, rotation and long standing; morning stiffness is common.</li>
      <li><strong>Sacroiliac joint (SIJ)</strong> — the joint between the spine and the pelvis. Pain low in the back on one side, radiating to the buttock and thigh.</li>
      <li><strong>A nerve root</strong> — with a <a href="/en/treatment/spine/disc-herniation/">disc herniation</a>. Pain shoots into the leg or arm, with possible numbness and weakness.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>A block helps only if the medication reaches the source of pain. So the source has to be found first — by examination and imaging.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="blokady">
    <h2>Types of blocks</h2>
    <p>A block is a targeted injection of two drugs: a <strong>local anaesthetic</strong>, which quickly relieves pain, and an <strong>anti-inflammatory steroid</strong>, which reduces inflammation and swelling and acts longer.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Paravertebral</strong> — into the tense muscles next to the spine. The simplest; helps with muscle pain. The effect often comes within 10–30 minutes.</li>
      <li><strong>Facet joint and SIJ block</strong> — precisely into the joint under X-ray or ultrasound guidance. For spinal joint arthritis and SIJ inflammation.</li>
      <li><strong>Radicular</strong> — to the inflamed nerve root under X-ray guidance. The only truly effective type of block for a disc herniation. <a href={`${tg}/49`} target="_blank" rel="noopener">More about the radicular block</a> is on my Telegram channel (in Russian).</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="kak">
    <h2>How a block is done</h2>
    <p>You lie on your stomach; the skin is cleaned with antiseptic and numbed. Under X-ray (C-arm) or ultrasound guidance a thin needle is guided precisely to the joint or root and the drugs are injected. The procedure takes a few minutes.</p>
    <p>A block is not the first step. Medication, exercise therapy and physiotherapy are tried first. A block is needed when pain persists despite them, and its task is to settle the flare-up and open a "window" for active rehabilitation.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="rcha">
    <h2>Radiofrequency ablation</h2>
    <p>If a facet or SIJ block helps but only briefly, the next step is <strong>radiofrequency ablation (RFA)</strong>. Through a thin needle under X-ray guidance, a probe is brought to the small nerve branches carrying pain from the joint, and a radiofrequency current is applied. The nerve is "switched off" from the pain circuit.</p>
    <ul>
      <li>under local anaesthesia, about 30 minutes;</li>
      <li>home the same day;</li>
      <li>the effect lasts from several months to a couple of years; the procedure can be repeated if needed.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>RFA is not used for pain from a disc herniation — it works specifically for pain from the spinal and pelvic joints.</p>
  </section>

  <section id="kogda">
    <h2>What is needed when</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Acute muscle pain</strong> — medication, movement, and a paravertebral block if needed.</li>
      <li><strong>Facet or SIJ pain</strong> — a targeted block; if the effect is short-lived — RFA.</li>
      <li><strong>Leg or arm pain from a herniation</strong> — a radicular block; if it does not help or weakness increases — we discuss removing the herniation.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section id="srochno" slot="urgent">
    <h2>When to seek urgent help</h2>
    <div class="callout alert">
      <p>Numbness in the groin area, bladder or bowel problems, rapidly increasing leg weakness — <strong>call emergency services immediately (112; 103 in Russia).</strong></p>
    </div>
  </section>
